Build succeeds. Here's the final code and explanation:

---

### Program.cs (stdio transport)

```csharp
var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// stdio uses stdout for JSON-RPC — all logs must go to stderr
builder.Logging.AddConsole(o => o.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register a typed HttpClient for the API wrapper tools
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();   // auto-discovers all [McpServerToolType] classes

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

### Tools/ApiTools.cs

The tool class uses `[McpServerToolType]` + `[McpServerTool]` + `[Description]` on every method and parameter so LLMs know what each tool does. Attributes like `ReadOnly = true` and `Destructive = false` give clients hints about side effects.

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

1. **Constructor injection** (used here) — make the tool class **non-static** with a primary constructor. Register services normally in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, etc.). The SDK instantiates the class per-request, resolving dependencies from the DI container.

2. **Method parameter injection** — make the class **static**. DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool parameters. The SDK distinguishes them automatically: only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the tool schema; DI types (`HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<>`) are injected silently.

**Key rule:** `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, and any DI-registered service **never** appear in the tool's JSON schema — only `[Description]`-annotated parameters are visible to the LLM.

The project is at `/tmp/sv-f099d2a1d5064f16b9fbfce116ede506/ApiMcpServer` and builds cleanly. Update the `BaseAddress` in `Program.cs` to point to your real API.
